`,c.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Overview"}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.p,{children:["LLMs are great at generating code, but the highest leverage comes from using them as a ",c.jsx(t.em,{children:"workflow amplifier"}),": write small tools, propose experiments, and help you isolate problems."]}),`
`,c.jsx(t.p,{children:"This post is organized into three parts:"}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"an overview of the mindset"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"the concrete benefits (where LLMs save time)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"the predictable pitfalls (“坑”) and how to avoid them"}),`
`]}),`
`,c.jsx(t.h2,{children:"Benefits"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.h3,{children:"1) For derived-data bugs: ask for lineage + a probe plan"}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Some bugs are not “hard to fix” — they’re hard to ",c.jsx(t.em,{children:"locate"}),"."]}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.p,{children:["If a computed metric is wrong but it is produced through hundreds (or thousands) of lines of transformation code, humans do not scale by mentally simulating the pipeline. Instead, use the model to generate a ",c.jsx(t.strong,{children:"hypothesis shortlist"})," plus an ",c.jsx(t.strong,{children:"instrumentation plan"}),"."]}),`
`,c.jsx(t.p,{children:"What to provide:"}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"one concrete failing example (raw input + wrong output)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"what the output should be (or at least an invariant it violates)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"environment context (timezone/locale/config/precision)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"any known-good intermediate values"}),`
`]}),`
`,c.jsx(t.p,{children:"What to demand back:"}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"a quick lineage sketch (where the value likely gets transformed)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"top hypotheses (unit mismatch, time boundary, rounding, missing data, state leaks, join mistakes)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"probe points (where to log/assert, and what to record)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.li,{children:"a minimal repro test/script skeleton"}),`
`]}),`
`,c.jsx(t.p,{children:"This keeps you in an experiment loop: add discriminating probes → rerun → narrow → fix → add a regression test."}),`
`,c.jsx(t.h3,{children:"2) Cut dependency chains with LLM-generated stubs (especially in Python)"}),`
`,c.jsx(t.p,{children:"Debugging often fails for boring reasons: you can’t even get the system into a runnable state because the dependency chain is too long."}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In high-coupling Python codebases, a simple ",c.jsx(t.code,{children:"from a import x"})," can drag in a huge import graph and fail on unrelated packages or import-time side effects."]}),`
`,c.jsxs(t.p,{children:["LLMs help by quickly producing ",c.jsx(t.strong,{children:"minimal shims"})," that satisfy imports and isolate the unit you want to test. A very effective technique is to inject a stub module via ",c.jsx(t.code,{children:"sys.modules"})," ",c.jsx(t.em,{children:"before"})," importing the target:"]}),`
